    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d702">

                <pb facs="0744" n="486" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg486"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1386b</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Swedish Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ekengren</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 26, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Minister</hi>: I have received your
                    letter delivered by Miss Hendrickson, the Chancellor of
                    your Legation, in regard to negotiations between the United States and Great
                    Britain upon the question of neutral rights.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d702-f1"/> As
                    I informed Miss Hendrickson, the Department has just
                    received a note from Great Britain in reply to the United States note of March
                    30, relative to the order in council of March 11.</p>
                <p>I have not, however, had time to read the note which has just been placed on my
                    desk and am, therefore, unable to say at the present moment what the attitude of
                    this Government will be. Of course, this Government will prepare a reply to this
                    British note, and mean-while I shall be glad to discuss with you personally the
                    points of difference between your Government and the British Government, with a
                    view to ascertaining whether it is possible for our two Governments to make more
                    or less uniform representations to the British Government in behalf of the
                    rights of neutrals.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
